<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Red Bike.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations. Interactive: Identify objects in the immediate environment in response to simple questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize colors and transportation-related words, such as "car" and "bus," in simple announcements with visual aids (e.g., circling the picture of a "red car" when it is mentioned in a short visual announcement). Can follow simple instructions related to transportation items as heard in simple stories (e.g., "Find the green car."). Can respond to simple questions about transportation vehicles by pointing or naming them (e.g., "What color is the car?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and concepts for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large, colorful flashcards of a red bike, a blue car, and a green bus. The teacher points to each flashcard and says, "Look! A red bike! That's a red bike!" then "Look! A blue car! That's a blue car!" and "Look! A green bus! That's a green bus!" Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher leads a simple TPR activity by saying "Red bike, go!" and students pretend to ride a bike; "Blue car, stop!" and students pretend to stop a car. This activity connects to the theme "That's a Red Bike." and activates prior knowledge of colors and general vehicles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language "It is a [color] [vehicle]." using flashcards. For example, holding up a red bike flashcard: "This is a bike. It is a red bike." (Teacher points to the bike, then to the color red.) The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /k/ in "car" and /b/ in "bus" while modeling, exaggerating the initial sounds. Teacher also models polite requests: "Please, point to the red bike."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visual cues and simple questions:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a blue car flashcard) "Is it a red bike or a blue car?" (Students point to the correct picture or make a single-word response if able).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a green bus flashcard) "What color is it? Red or green?" (Students point to the color or say "Green!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>0-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language/content related to the scenario "Colors of Things that Go."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio announcement/story (e.g., recorded by the teacher or an app) accompanied by large, clear visual aids (e.g., a storyboard with 3-4 panels depicting different colored vehicles going/stopping). The genre is a short descriptive story, and the register is simple and informal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces a poster with 4 pictures: a red bike, a blue car, a green bus, and a yellow taxi.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short audio story/announcement (e.g., "Listen! Look! I see a red bike. It goes fast! Vroom, vroom! I see a blue car. It goes and stops. Beep, beep! Oh! A green bus! It is big. And a yellow taxi!"). The teacher points to the corresponding pictures as the audio plays.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> (Teacher replays parts of the audio as needed)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the story about? Toys or vehicles?" (Students point to the vehicles on the poster or say "Vehicles!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching/Identifying:</b> The teacher asks, "What color is the bike?" (Students point to the red bike picture). "What color is the bus?" (Students point to the green bus picture). "Point to the yellow taxi."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> The teacher says, "The car is red." (Students look at the blue car picture and shake their heads "no" or point down). "The bus is green." (Students look at the green bus picture and nod "yes" or point up).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>10-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy-focused activities for listening comprehension, targeting specific language forms.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening to and identifying colors and vehicle names. Grammar: Color + noun (e.g., "green car"), WH-questions (e.g., "What color is the car?"), Present simple (e.g., "It is a red bike."). Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi, green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange. Pronunciation: /k/ in "car" and /b/ in "bus," initial sound /y/ in "yellow." Pragmatic: Answering questions about colors and objects by pointing or one-word answers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Point" (Whole Class):</b> The teacher spreads flashcards of various colored vehicles (e.g., red bike, blue car, green bus, yellow taxi, orange truck) on the floor or whiteboard. The teacher says instructions like, "Please, point to the red bike." or "Point to the blue car." Students listen and point to the correct flashcard. The teacher repeats, varying the order and speed. The teacher might ask, "What color is the car?" and students point to the blue car.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Listen and Circle the Sound" (Individual Worksheet):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pairs of pictures, for example, a "car" and a "bus," and then a "yellow" object and a "red" object. The teacher says a word, like "car." Students listen and circle the picture that starts with the /k/ sound. Then, the teacher says "bus" and students circle the picture starting with the /b/ sound. Later, the teacher says "yellow" and students circle the picture starting with the /y/ sound.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction: "Listen, Find, and Show":</b> Students work in pairs with a small set of colored vehicle flashcards (e.g., red bike, blue car, green bus, yellow taxi). The teacher gives an instruction: "Student A, listen: Please, find the green bus. Show it to your partner." "Student B, listen: Please, find the yellow taxi. Show it to your partner." This activity promotes active listening and simple identification, with the peer confirming the action.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>20-35 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario "Colors of Things that Go."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Listen and Arrange My Traffic Scene" (Small Groups/Pairs):</b> Students work in small groups or pairs. Each group receives cut-outs of various colored vehicles (e.g., a red bike, a blue car, a green bus, a yellow taxi, a purple train, an orange truck) and a simple drawing of a road/city background. The teacher acts as the "traffic controller" giving instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Okay, listen carefully! First, please put the red bike on the road. That's a red bike." Students listen and place the red bike cutout.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, "Now, put the blue car next to the red bike. It is a blue car."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, "Please, put the green bus behind the blue car. The bus is big!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the taxi? Yes, yellow! Put the yellow taxi on the road."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher continues to give 3-4 more instructions, using different vehicles and colors from the vocabulary list (truck, train, purple, orange), focusing on the prepositions (on) and descriptive phrases (It is a...). The students listen and arrange their cut-outs to create their traffic scene.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After all instructions, the teacher asks general comprehension questions, "What color is your train?" and students point to their purple train.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>35-45 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Listen and Arrange" and a short "Listen and Circle" worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a simple worksheet with drawings of different colored vehicles (e.g., a red bike, a blue car, a green bus, a yellow taxi, an orange truck). The teacher reads simple instructions/statements:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen and circle the red bike."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen and draw a line to the green bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen and point to the yellow taxi. What color is it?" (Teacher allows student to point to the taxi and say "Yellow" or simply point.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen and color the blue car."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes vehicle names when heard (car, bus, bike, taxi, truck).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies colors when heard (red, blue, green, yellow, orange).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows 1-step oral instructions (e.g., circle, draw a line, point, color).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "happy face" or "sad face" card (or thumbs up/down) in response to:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the colors (red, blue, green)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the cars and buses."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>45-50 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Learner and teacher reflection, and connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the class (allowing for pointing or simple gestures/words):</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new colors did you hear today? (Point to flashcards as options if needed)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vehicles did you hear today? (e.g., car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi - show flashcards for recall)."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked particularly well in teaching listening comprehension for this age group? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, short audio segments, repeating instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to ensure all students are engaged and comprehending? (e.g., more direct pronunciation practice, simpler vocabulary, more repetition of core phrases).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the integration of grammar (Color + noun, WH-questions) and vocabulary be reinforced more effectively through listening activities?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Great job listening to all the colorful vehicles today! Next time, we will look at some pictures and read a very short story about these same cars, bikes, and buses. We will see them in a book!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>End of Lesson</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>